# BUGS

fsd serves one request at a time, so a request that waits for the disk delays every client; it attaches at most 16 branches in a run and keeps each until it exits, and opens at most 32 snapshots, through labels and the dump view together, until it restarts.
Threads and the pipelined server of M6 step 6d are to lift these.

Who a client is stays advisory until keyd (M10), and POSIX programs see every file owned by user 0 until M6 step 6e.

Nothing makes dated snapshots or drops them: the retention policy of `docs/06-install-update.md` §10, with `/home` snapshotted before an update, has no step yet, and `/home` and `/cfg` are snapshotted only by hand and by distd's `apply`.
No namespace of an installed system has `/home` or `/cfg` but one whose manifest mounts it; the console shell's has neither.

poweroff(8) stops no service first, so fsd is not halted and a power-off is a crash, which loses the last seconds of changes; an orderly shutdown through svcd is M6 step 6g's.
drv-nvme trusts a device's volatile write cache to survive a reset, until 6g flushes it first on a real disk.

A hostile volume image can make fsd free live blocks or spin; only the system volume, which the system made, is mounted, and a fuzz target that mounts crafted images is 6d's.

partd and install take disks of 512-byte sectors only.
A volume cannot grow, and has one device: no mirroring, compression or encryption, for which the format leaves room.
There is no exFAT, and no ext4 even read-only.
The design's `fsd -c`, checking an unmounted volume, and `fsd -r`, making one, do not exist.
